<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Angrivarii</h1><p class="st-meta">1 CE – 300 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Germanic people of the middle Weser whose earthen boundary wall became the site of a major battle between Germanicus and Arminius in 16 CE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Angrivarii lived on both banks of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>, north of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cherusci">Cherusci</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>. They appear in Roman sources around the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero-claudius-drusus">Drusus</a>' campaigns, and were at times allies, at times enemies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of their government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> mentions a broad earthwork they had built to mark their boundary with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cherusci">Cherusci</a>; in 16 CE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-angrivarian-wall">Battle of the Angrivarian Wall</a> there after his victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-idistaviso">Idistaviso</a>. After a revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> they submitted and returned Roman captives bought from other tribes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 98 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> reports that the Angrivarii and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chamavi">Chamavi</a> had moved into the lands of the defeated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bructeri">Bructeri</a>. They were later absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saxons">Saxons</a>; their name survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angria">Engern</a> (Angria), one of the three provinces of Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastphalia">Eastphalia</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/angrivarii.jpg" alt="Angrivarii" width="320" height="162"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Lancaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lower_Rhine_10BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1 CE – 300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Germanic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Weser-Rhine Germanic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=angrivarii" aria-label="Angrivarii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4896 414 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Angrivarii lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5216 670h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5216 670h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Germany. <a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=angrivarii">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
